      <memberdef kind="function" id="classNeoN_1_1BoundaryData_1a2584e1a22b4fb981b283dbd6d7cab160" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="yes" virt="non-virtual">
        <type><ref refid="classNeoN_1_1Vector" kindref="compound">Vector</ref>&lt; ValueType &gt; &amp;</type>
        <definition>Vector&lt; ValueType &gt; &amp; NeoN::BoundaryData&lt; ValueType &gt;::valueNoWait</definition>
        <argsstring>()</argsstring>
        <name>valueNoWait</name>
        <qualifiedname>NeoN::BoundaryData::valueNoWait</qualifiedname>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Non-draining access to the value storage. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><simplesect kind="warning"><para>Does NOT call <ref refid="classNeoN_1_1BoundaryData_1abe68f27d45b391253709669681e40528" kindref="member">waitAll()</ref>, so any in-flight processor-halo exchange is left pending. Used by the processor boundary condition to SEED the owner value before posting its isend/irecv: seeding must NOT drain a previous patch&apos;s exchange, because completing and clearing the comm buffers patch-by-patch (mid correctBoundaryConditions loop) serialises the halo exchange and, on a rank with two proc patches, leaves the second patch&apos;s recv unmatched — the halo then silently keeps its owner seed. All patches must post first; the single <ref refid="classNeoN_1_1BoundaryData_1abe68f27d45b391253709669681e40528" kindref="member">waitAll()</ref> triggered by the next real <ref refid="classNeoN_1_1BoundaryData_1aa0ac1c738252c54d92ec822832765d3f" kindref="member">value()</ref> read then completes them together. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
